{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at head ca7d692d: the cold audit can say how far it has got, and the browser can say what the wait is.\n\nA resident rebuilding from cold serves nothing for minutes while the shell and hashed assets arrive instantly from a path that takes no lock. That is what a fold-profile bump looks like from outside: a finished, empty page. Indistinguishable from a broken bundle, which is how it was reported.\n\nNothing could report on it because the work runs under two locks. Workspace.SnapshotWithSource holds snapshotMu for its whole call and Reader.Load holds r.mu across scanHead, so every reader that arrives queues behind the audit rather than being told one is happening. Progress therefore cannot travel through either mutex — a value guarded by the lock the slow work holds can never be read while the slow work runs. The verification loop publishes into atomics; Reader.AuditProgress and Workspace.RebuildProgress read them without taking anything.\n\n/v0/rebuild answers while Snapshot is blocked, which is the whole difference between a slow page and one that looks broken. RebuildNotice polls it and renders nothing at all when the resident is warm, so the ordinary brief wait still reads as a wait.\n\nThis touches the kernel, and the shape is chosen to keep that as small as possible: the counter is write-only from the verifier's side, no control flow reads it, and deleting it would change no decision about what is accepted. It reports on the audit; it is not part of it.\n\nFour tests, each pinning a different thing. The counter is readable while the audit holds the lock, with the poller deliberately never joined — joining would turn a wrong design into a hung test rather than a failing one, which is exactly what my first draft did, timing out at ten minutes. The endpoint answers while a snapshot is in flight. The notice reports a measured proportion and must not match error, failed, broken, up to date, or current as of. And the profile mismatch reproduces the reported failure end to end, asserting loaded.Full so it cannot pass vacuously on a warm path.\n\nOne condition is met by refusing it, and that is the honest reading rather than a shortfall. The request allows serving a previously verified projection under a rebuilding qualifier. After a profile bump there is no such projection: the prior checkpoint is invalid under a contract this binary no longer implements. Progress with no work data is what the request already permits for first startup; stale rows behind a reassuring label would satisfy the letter and invert the point.\n\nGates at this head: `go vet ./...` clean; `go test ./internal/... ./cmd/...` exits 0 across 15 packages; race detector clean on the progress test; `npx tsc --noEmit` clean; UI suite 73/73; `make ui-check` green with the embed regenerated; clean worktree; current main 8f22ba52 is an ancestor.","body":{"commit":"ca7d692dd51889c2b942e7e8794fef19a0d6fcf6","path":"internal/kernel"}}